workedExamples.solution_text:
Find the projection first.
$\begin{aligned}\underset{\sim}{a}\cdot\underset{\sim}{b}&=4(2)+7(1)=15\\|\underset{\sim}{b}|^2&=2^2+1^2=5\\\text{proj}_{\underset{\sim}{b}}\underset{\sim}{a}&=\frac{15}{5}\begin{pmatrix}2\\1\end{pmatrix}=\begin{pmatrix}6\\3\end{pmatrix}\end{aligned}$
Subtract the projection from the original vector.
$\begin{aligned}\underset{\sim}{a}-\text{proj}_{\underset{\sim}{b}}\underset{\sim}{a}&=\begin{pmatrix}4\\7\end{pmatrix}-\begin{pmatrix}6\\3\end{pmatrix}\\&=\begin{pmatrix}-2\\4\end{pmatrix}=2\begin{pmatrix}-1\\2\end{pmatrix}\end{aligned}$
The directions $\begin{pmatrix}2\\1\end{pmatrix}$ and $\begin{pmatrix}-1\\2\end{pmatrix}$ are perpendicular. Confirm both checks:
$\begin{aligned}\begin{pmatrix}-2\\4\end{pmatrix}\cdot\begin{pmatrix}2\\1\end{pmatrix}&=-4+4=0\\\begin{pmatrix}6\\3\end{pmatrix}+\begin{pmatrix}-2\\4\end{pmatrix}&=\begin{pmatrix}4\\7\end{pmatrix}\end{aligned}$
The blue projection followed by the red perpendicular component reaches the same endpoint as the original vector.
